<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; border: 1px solid #a7d9ed; padding: 15px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Arts and Crafts – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em; color: #333;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Arts and Crafts</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> This Is a Local Artist.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Describe a favorite hobby. Interactive: Role-play everyday situations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe a favorite craft or art activity (e.g., "My favorite craft is making baskets. First, you prepare the materials and soak the fibers"). Can role-play conversations about buying art (e.g., "I'd like to buy this tagua carving. How much does it cost?"). Can discuss local art in a conversation with peers (e.g., "This artist has created beautiful sculptures").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays pictures of various local Panamanian crafts and artworks (e.g., molas, tagua carvings, traditional baskets, murals) on the board. Students are asked to verbally identify them and share if they know any artists who create similar pieces. Teacher prompts: "What do you see in these pictures?" "Have you ever seen a mola being made?" "Do you know any artists from our community?" (Speaking: activate prior knowledge and vocabulary).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models short sentences using target vocabulary and grammar to describe one of the crafts. E.g., "This is a mola. An indigenous artist has created this colorful design." "My favorite craft is making baskets. First, you prepare the materials." The teacher also models the pronunciation of 'sculpture' (/skʌlp.tʃər/) and 'design' (/dɪ'zaɪn/), focusing on syllable stress.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key terms:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a 'sculpture' something you wear or something you look at?" (Expected answer: "Something you look at.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If an artist 'carves' something, do they paint it or cut into it?" (Expected answer: "Cut into it.")</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ / ___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Arts and Crafts".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio dialogue between two friends, Carla and Luis, visiting a local art gallery and discussing different artworks and the artists. The dialogue incorporates descriptions of creations, artists' achievements, and art-related vocabulary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Express ideas, feelings, and information in simple terms about personal experiences and immediate surroundings related to local art and artists.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Oral Response):</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> After listening to the dialogue once, students answer orally: "Where are Carla and Luis?" "What kind of art are they talking about?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Detail questions:</b> After listening a second time, students answer in pairs: "What has the artist, Maria, created recently?" (Expected: "She has created beautiful sculptures.") "What adjective do they use to describe the tagua carving?" (Expected: "Unique," "detailed"). Focus on identifying inflectional endings for past tense verbs in 'created' and gerunds in 'carving'.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ / ___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Present perfect (e.g., "This artist has created beautiful sculptures."), Present and past simple to describe life and achievements (e.g., "Yesterday, we visited an artist who makes molas."), Imperatives to explain how to create something (e.g., "First, you prepare the materials and soak the fibers.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> artist, craft, basket, sculpture, gallery, studio, exhibition, canvas, building, mural, mola, tagua carving; create, paint, sculpt, design, carve, display; creative, artistic, handmade, colorful, abstract, bold, vibrant, minimalist, timeless, unique, detailed; with, from, by; skillfully, originally.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Syllable stress in 'sculpture' (/skʌlp.tʃər/), 'design' (/dɪ'zaɪn/). Identification and articulation of inflectional endings (-ed, -ing).</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                        <ol>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Sentence Starters - Oral):</b> The teacher provides sentence starters on the board related to local artists and their work. Students complete the sentences orally in pairs using the target grammar and vocabulary. E.g., "This artist has ______ (create) many ______ (sculpture)." "Yesterday, I ______ (visit) a ______ (gallery)." "My favorite craft is ______ (make) ______ (basket)." Teacher circulates, providing feedback on accuracy and pronunciation.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice (Q&A Chains):</b> Students form small groups. One student shows a picture of a craft (e.g., mola, tagua carving). The first student asks "What is this?" The second student answers, e.g., "This is a mola. An artist from the Guna Yala community has designed it." The third student asks, "How is it created?" and the fourth responds using imperatives, e.g., "First, you draw geometric shapes. Then, you cut out the fabric." (Focus on describing creations and how to create something).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction (Describing a Hobby):</b> Students work in pairs. Each student describes a favorite craft or art activity they enjoy or know about, focusing on using "My favorite craft is..." and imperatives to explain the steps, and past/present simple to talk about experiences. "My favorite craft is painting. First, you get a canvas. Then, you choose colorful paints. Last week, I painted a bold mural." Partner listens and asks clarifying questions.</li>
                        </ol>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ / ___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Arts and Crafts".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task Format:</b> Role-play: "Visiting a Local Artist's Studio/Gallery."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task Description:</b> Students work in pairs. One student plays the role of a local artist or gallery owner, and the other plays a visitor interested in buying art. The artist/owner describes their work or the work of local artists (using present perfect for achievements, adjectives for descriptions, and potentially imperatives to explain a process). The visitor asks questions about the artworks (e.g., "I'd like to buy this tagua carving. How much does it cost? Who has carved this unique piece?"), expressing interest and negotiating. They discuss at least three different pieces.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Connection to Theme:</b> Directly addresses "This Is a Local Artist." and "Arts and Crafts" by simulating an authentic interaction related to local art.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency, collaborative interaction, communicative task completion, and spontaneous use of target grammar and vocabulary to describe creations, achievements, and engage in a realistic conversation.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ / ___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist. The teacher uses a simple checklist to observe pairs during the role-play, noting students' ability to produce target language.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the "Visiting a Local Artist's Studio/Gallery" role-play in front of the teacher or record it (if technology allows).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation, especially of syllable stress in art-related vocabulary and correct articulation of -ed/-ing endings.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (nouns, verbs, adjectives).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate use of Present Perfect, Present Simple, and Imperatives.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence in describing creations, achievements, and explaining processes.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active engagement and collaboration in the role-play.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a short exit ticket:
                        <ol>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Circle one: I can describe a craft / I can describe an artist's achievement / I can role-play buying art."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word or phrase I used successfully today was: _______."</li>
                        </ol>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ / ___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection on the lesson's effectiveness and future planning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Student Self-reflection:</b> The teacher asks students to reflect orally or in their notebooks:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of describing art or artists did you find easiest today?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part was more challenging, and how can you improve it next time?"</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">What worked well in this lesson? The role-play activity significantly boosted speaking confidence, and students effectively used the present perfect to describe artists' achievements. The visual prompts in the warm-up were engaging.</p>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? Some students still struggle with distinguishing between present simple and present perfect when describing general facts vs. past experiences. More focused practice on inflectional endings (-ed) is needed. I will prepare additional sentence frames for the next lesson to support sentence construction.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "In our next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition), we will use all the vocabulary and grammar we practiced speaking today to write a descriptive paragraph about a local artist and their creations, or even how to create a simple craft."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ / ___ min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>